! ElasticSolve reusing the WHOLE assembled system -- "Constant System", the
! third and boldest of the three reuse depths.
!
!   Constant Bulk Matrix -- the matrix is reused, the load is reassembled
!   Constant Bulk System -- matrix and bulk load reused, only the BCs run
!   Constant System      -- nothing is assembled at all
!
! Each is behaviour-neutral by construction, so what the four RUNs below pin is
! exactly that: enabling a keyword must not move the answer. That is worth a test
! even though it cannot be told from the feature silently not engaging, because
! the failure mode it does catch is the dangerous one -- restoring the system
! from the wrong point and dropping a contribution.
!
! Hence the Neumann condition on boundary 2 rather than a prescribed
! displacement. The bulk values are saved before the boundary assembly runs, so
! a "Constant System" that skipped the boundary conditions while restoring only
! the bulk state would silently lose that force. With Dirichlet conditions alone
! the boundary assembly contributes nothing and the error would not show.
!
! Two steady state iterations per RUN, since with one there is no previous pass
! and nothing is reused.

Header
  Mesh DB "." "square"
End

Simulation
  Coordinate System = Cartesian 2D
  Simulation Type = Steady State
  Steady State Max Iterations = 2
  Steady State Min Iterations = 2
End

Body 1
  Equation = 1
  Material = 1
End

Material 1
  Youngs Modulus = 1.0e3
  Poisson Ratio = 0.3
  Density = 1.0
End

Equation 1
  Active Solvers(1) = 1
End

Solver 1
  Equation = "Elasticity"
  Procedure = "ElasticSolve" "ElasticSolver"
  Variable = -dofs 2 Displacement

  ! The reuse is only sound for a linear problem; the solver refuses the
  ! nonlinear paths outright rather than assemble a stale tangent.
  Large Deflection = False

  Linear System Solver = Direct
  Linear System Direct Method = UMFPack
  Nonlinear System Max Iterations = 1
End

Boundary Condition 1
  Target Boundaries(1) = 1
  Displacement 1 = Real 0.0
  Displacement 2 = Real 0.0
End

Boundary Condition 2
  Target Boundaries(1) = 3
  Force 1 = Real 5.0
  Force 2 = Real 3.0
End

! Its own directory, and that is the finding rather than tidiness. It cannot be a
! further RUN of ElasticConstantSystem: Solver % Matrix % BulkValues persists
! across RUNs and setting the narrower keywords False does not discard it, so
! this level would restore a bulk-only system saved under the previous regime and
! silently lose the boundary force. Its save point is after the boundary
! assembly; theirs is before it.
!
! The keyword stands on its own here. It did not used to: the full system is
! saved only in the boundary assembly slot, and the test for it there was nested
! inside the "Calculate Loads" one, so on its own nothing was ever saved and no
! solver relying on that path could engage. Corrected in DefUtils along with this
! test -- MagnetoDynamics was never affected, passing the flag explicitly instead.
!
! The Neumann condition on boundary 2 is what gives the test its teeth: skipping
! the boundary assembly while restoring from the wrong point would drop that
! force. With Dirichlet conditions alone the error could not show.

Solver 1 :: Constant System = Logical True
Solver 1 :: Reference Norm = Real 1.70786776E-03
RUN

! Proof that the reuse actually engages, which nothing above can give: every
! level is behaviour-neutral by construction, so a reference norm cannot tell a
! working reuse from one that quietly never fires -- as this test itself did not,
! until this RUN was added.
!
! So assert constancy falsely, on purpose. Solver % Matrix % BulkValues persists
! across RUNs, so a working reuse restores the system assembled above and ignores
! the tenfold stiffer material below entirely, reproducing the same norm. Were
! nothing being saved, this RUN would reassemble and land near a tenth of it.
Material 1 :: Youngs Modulus = Real 1.0e4
Solver 1 :: Reference Norm = Real 1.70786776E-03
RUN
